Searches covered Russian negotiating statements and draft status, Putin’s treatment of a unit implicated at Bucha, Wagner/Concord financing, and Russian remedial or investigative conduct. The strongest additions are: Russia’s contemporaneous rejection of Ukraine’s 6 April draft, which independently confirms that no complete settlement had been mutually accepted; Putin’s personal commendation of the 64th Motor Rifle Brigade after public Bucha allegations; and Putin’s later distinction between Wagner’s state financing and Concord’s potentially questionable state-contract earnings. Reuters preserved the Russian and Ukrainian positions in the same report. [Reuters via Euronews](https://www.euronews.com/2022/04/08/us-ukraine-crisis-russia-lavrov-agreement)

No independently checkable Russian prosecution or discipline of Russian personnel for the documented 2022 killings was recovered. The only concrete Russian-initiated remedial step found was its request, alongside Ukraine, for a UN inquiry into Olenivka; that is narrow contrary evidence, and it did not cure the wider accountability gap. The ICRC reported that Russia-controlled authorities still had not granted requested access. [UN appointment notice](https://www.un.org/sg/en/content/sg/note-correspondents/2022-08-22/note-correspondents-secretary-general-appoints-members-of-fact-finding-mission-regarding-incident-olenivka-29-july-2022), [ICRC access account](https://www.icrc.org/en/document/olenivka-penal-facility-prisoners-war-and-icrcs-role)

Complete, officially attributable 2022 Russian draft-treaty texts remain unrecovered; consequently, no source supports treating the Istanbul materials as a final mutually accepted settlement. Evidence of Putin’s advance knowledge of particular affiliated-force abuses also remains unavailable: the brigade commendation establishes tolerance or non-correction after allegations became public, not prior knowledge or an abuse order. Wagner records distinguish military financing and potentially patronage-bearing contracts, but do not establish Putin’s personal enrichment.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Russia says Ukraine presented 'unacceptable' draft peace deal","publisher":"Reuters, republished by Euronews","publication_date":"2022-04-08","url":"https://www.euronews.com/2022/04/08/us-ukraine-crisis-russia-lavrov-agreement","claim":"On 7 April 2022 Russian Foreign Minister Sergey Lavrov publicly said Ukraine's 6 April draft contained unacceptable departures from earlier negotiating understandings, specifically concerning presidential consideration of Crimea and foreign military exercises in Ukraine, while saying Russia would continue negotiations using a draft reflecting its own demands. Ukraine negotiator Mykhailo Podolyak disputed Lavrov's characterization. The opposing contemporaneous accounts establish that material terms remained contested and that no complete draft had been mutually accepted.","locator":"Article lines 642-682, especially 658, 665-676 and 681-682","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C2-001","canonical_fact_key":"putin-2022-istanbul-april-6-draft-remained-disputed","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.7"],"source_type":"contemporaneous_wire_report_with_attributable_statements","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Reuters reports named, contemporaneous statements from both sides and identifies the concrete provisions in dispute; neither underlying draft is reproduced, so the record establishes disagreement rather than the drafts' complete contents.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Directly contemporaneous with the April 2022 negotiations.","ruler_attribution":"Lavrov stated the Russian government's negotiating position; this is attributable to Putin's government but is not evidence that Putin personally authored every term.","contrary_evidence":"Lavrov asserted that earlier understandings existed and that Russia intended to continue negotiating; Podolyak rejected Lavrov's account as propaganda. Neither statement establishes final mutual acceptance.","underlying_fact_key":"putin-2022-istanbul-april-6-draft-remained-disputed","lenses":["2B.7"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Putin honours brigade accused by Ukraine of ‘war crimes’ in Bucha","publisher":"Al Jazeera, reporting news-agency material and the presidential decree","publication_date":"2022-04-19","url":"https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2022/4/19/putin-honours-brigade-accused-by-ukraine-of-war-crimes-in-bucha","claim":"On 18 April 2022 Putin signed a decree granting the 64th Motor Rifle Brigade the title 'Guards' and praising its mass heroism, courage, resolve and skill during the Ukraine operation. This occurred after Ukrainian authorities had publicly accused the brigade of Bucha abuses and after civilian bodies had been documented there. The timing establishes Putin's personal commendation rather than investigation or correction of an accused unit, but does not by itself prove the brigade committed each alleged crime or that Putin knew of particular abuses before they occurred.","locator":"Headline and standfirst; paragraphs at lines 75-90, especially lines 77-81 and 90","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C2-002","canonical_fact_key":"putin-2022-commended-64th-brigade-after-bucha-allegations","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.5","2B.9"],"source_type":"contemporaneous_reporting_on_presidential_decree","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"The commendation and decree language are specific and directly attributable to Putin; the linkage of the brigade to Bucha was then an allegation by Ukrainian authorities, so the claim carefully separates the official award from unresolved individual criminal attribution.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"The decree and public allegations both occurred in April 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct: Putin personally signed the decree and commended the brigade after the allegations were public.","contrary_evidence":"The Kremlin denied Russian responsibility and described Bucha evidence as fake. The source does not establish Putin's advance knowledge, a command to abuse civilians, or the criminal responsibility of every brigade member.","underlying_fact_key":"putin-2022-commended-64th-brigade-after-bucha-allegations","lenses":["2B.5","2B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Putin reveals plans to scrutinize Concord company funds","publisher":"Interfax","publication_date":"2023-06-27","url":"https://www.interfax-russia.ru/military/news_eng/395954","claim":"Putin stated that the Russian state fully supported Wagner while Prigozhin's Concord company separately received or earned 80 billion rubles through Voentorg food-supply and army-catering work. He said authorities would examine whether funds had been stolen. This distinguishes state financing of Wagner's military capacity from a separate stream of potentially patronage-bearing commercial revenue; it raises, but does not prove, diversion or personal enrichment.","locator":"Article lines 274-280, especially paragraphs at lines 277-279","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C2-003","canonical_fact_key":"putin-2022-wagner-financing-distinct-from-concord-contract-revenue","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.8"],"source_type":"later_wire_report_of_presidential_statement_establishing_2022_financial_facts","source_confidence":"medium","source_confidence_reason":"The statement is directly attributed to Putin and separates two funding streams, but it was made after Wagner's 2023 mutiny and provides neither an audited calendar-2022 breakdown nor results of the promised scrutiny.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Published in 2023 but specifically addresses state financing and contracting overlapping May-December 2022.","ruler_attribution":"Direct for Putin's knowledge and characterization of the funding arrangements; no source establishes that he personally received the money.","contrary_evidence":"Putin framed the 80 billion rubles as payment earned for actual food and catering services and merely proposed checking for theft. The statement therefore does not establish corrupt diversion, personal enrichment, or that patronage rather than military purpose motivated Wagner funding.","underlying_fact_key":"putin-2022-wagner-financing-distinct-from-concord-contract-revenue","lenses":["2B.8"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Secretary-General appoints members of Fact-finding Mission regarding Incident at Olenivka on 29 July 2022","publisher":"United Nations Secretary-General","publication_date":"2022-08-22","url":"https://www.un.org/sg/en/content/sg/note-correspondents/2022-08-22/note-correspondents-secretary-general-appoints-members-of-fact-finding-mission-regarding-incident-olenivka-29-july-2022","claim":"The UN Secretary-General established a fact-finding mission into the 29 July 2022 Olenivka incident following requests from both Russia and Ukraine and appointed three named members. Russia's request is narrow contrary evidence to a claim of total refusal to seek any external inquiry, but the mission was fact-finding rather than a Russian criminal or disciplinary proceeding and produced no 2022 accountability finding.","locator":"Opening two paragraphs under the title, beginning 'On 3 August 2022' and listing the three mission members","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C2-004","canonical_fact_key":"russia-ukraine-2022-joint-requests-un-olenivka-inquiry","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.9"],"source_type":"official_un_procedural_record","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"The UN directly records receipt of both governments' requests and the mission's appointment; it does not attribute culpability or document a Russian prosecution.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly concerns Russian conduct in August 2022 and a July 2022 incident.","ruler_attribution":"The request is attributable to the Russian government, not shown to have been personally ordered by Putin.","contrary_evidence":"This is a concrete Russian request for an international fact-finding process, but it is event-specific and cannot substitute for investigative or disciplinary records concerning Russian personnel implicated in other abuses.","underlying_fact_key":"russia-ukraine-2022-joint-requests-un-olenivka-inquiry","lenses":["2B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Olenivka penal facility: Prisoners of war and ICRC's role","publisher":"International Committee of the Red Cross","publication_date":"2022-08-03","url":"https://www.icrc.org/en/document/olenivka-penal-facility-prisoners-war-and-icrcs-role","claim":"The ICRC reported that immediately after the 29 July Olenivka incident it requested access to the site, victims, bodies and any facilities to which prisoners had been moved, but had not been granted access or the security guarantees required for a visit; it also said it had repeatedly requested access to POWs since its May visit. This qualifies Russia's request for a UN inquiry by showing that an established independent humanitarian monitor lacked contemporaneous access to verify conditions or remedial action.","locator":"Sections explaining the ICRC's 29 July access request, the absence of granted access and security guarantees, and repeated POW-access requests since May 2022","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C2-005","canonical_fact_key":"russia-controlled-olenivka-2022-independent-access-not-granted","disposition":"final_evidence","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.5","2B.9"],"source_type":"official_independent_humanitarian_record","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"The ICRC reports its own requests and access status, matters within its direct institutional knowledge; it does not determine responsibility for the explosion.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"Contemporaneous August 2022 account of access sought immediately after the July incident.","ruler_attribution":"The obstruction is attributable to authorities controlling Olenivka and relevant detention sites; the source does not establish a personal Putin instruction.","contrary_evidence":"Russia separately requested a UN fact-finding mission, so its conduct was not a categorical public refusal of outside inquiry. Nevertheless, the ICRC's missing access prevented prompt independent verification.","underlying_fact_key":"russia-controlled-olenivka-2022-independent-access-not-granted","lenses":["2B.5","2B.9"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Black Sea Grain Initiative signing remarks","publisher":"United Nations","publication_date":"2022-07-22","url":"https://press.un.org/en/2022/sgsm21381.doc.htm","claim":"Russia and Ukraine separately committed to the UN- and Türkiye-mediated Black Sea export mechanism on 22 July 2022. The signing is evidence of a limited, issue-specific wartime compromise and must be kept separate from the later renewal event; it does not establish acceptance of a general peace settlement.","locator":"Remarks identifying the Russian and Ukrainian representatives and welcoming their commitments at the 22 July signing ceremony","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C2-006","canonical_fact_key":"russia-ukraine-2022-grain-initiative-signing","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.7"],"source_type":"official_un_contemporaneous_record","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"The UN records the signing ceremony and parties' commitments directly; its scope was maritime food exports, not a comprehensive settlement.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly contemporaneous July 2022 event.","ruler_attribution":"Agreement by the Russian state is attributable to Putin's government, though this record does not show Putin personally negotiating or signing it.","contrary_evidence":"The agreement demonstrates that limited practical compromises were achievable despite the failure of broader peace negotiations.","underlying_fact_key":"russia-ukraine-2022-grain-initiative-signing","lenses":["2B.7"]}

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON:{"title":"Operational update: Black Sea Grain Initiative extended","publisher":"United Nations","publication_date":"2022-11-17","url":"https://www.un.org/en/black-sea-grain-initiative/operational-update-17-november-2022","claim":"The parties allowed continuation of the Black Sea Grain Initiative for a further 120 days beginning 19 November 2022. This November renewal is a distinct decision from the July signing and shows continued acceptance of a narrow operational bargain, not mutual acceptance of comprehensive settlement terms.","locator":"Opening operational-update paragraphs announcing continuation for 120 days from 19 November 2022","provisional_id":"WEB-2B-C2-007","canonical_fact_key":"russia-ukraine-2022-grain-initiative-november-renewal","disposition":"context","chapter_ids":["2B"],"methodology_ids":["2B.7"],"source_type":"official_un_contemporaneous_operational_record","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"The UN directly administered and reported the initiative's continuation; the record is precise about the duration and effective date.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"Directly contemporaneous November 2022 event.","ruler_attribution":"Russian consent is attributable to Putin's government; the source does not identify Putin as the individual decision-maker.","contrary_evidence":"The renewal is meaningful evidence of negotiated cooperation, but its limited subject matter cannot support an inference that territorial, security-guarantee or general ceasefire terms were accepted.","underlying_fact_key":"russia-ukraine-2022-grain-initiative-november-renewal","lenses":["2B.7"]}
